8. Trigger - Think of the Triggerer as a smart watcher that waits for things without wasting resources.

What problem it solves 
    Earlier in Airflow:
    A task (like a sensor) keeps checking:
    👉 “Has file arrived?”
    👉 “Is API ready?”
    ❌ While waiting → it holds a worker slot
    ❌ Worker just sits idle for hours → waste

    🚀 What Triggerer does
    👉 Instead of blocking a worker:
    Task says: “I’ll wait… but not here”
    It hands over waiting job to Triggerer

🔁 Simple flow
Task starts on worker
Task says: defer() (I’ll wait)
Worker is freed immediately ✅
Triggerer keeps checking condition
When ready → task goes back to worker and continues

9. Dag file - blueprint of your workflow.

A DAG file is just a Python script where you:
    1. Create a DAG (the workflow)
    👉 This is like naming and defining your pipeline
    DAG ID (name)
    Schedule (daily, hourly, etc.)
    Start date
    2. Define tasks (Operators)
    👉 These are the actual steps
    Examples:
    PythonOperator → run Python code
    BashOperator → run shell commands
    HttpOperator → call APIs
    👉 Each task = one unit of work
    3. Set dependencies (order)
    Decide which task runs first
    task1 >> task2
    4. Use Connections & Variables

Best practices
    1. Keep DAG file lightweight
        ❌ Don’t do heavy work inside DAG file
        ✔️ Only define structure
        👉 Because Airflow reads it frequently
    2. Use Git
    3. Use decorators (@dag, @task)
    4. Parameterize (reuse DAG)
        don't hardcode values

Key Elements
    1. DAG object — defines ID, schedule, start date, and defaults
    2. Operators — PythonOperator, BashOperator, HttpOperator, etc.
    3. Dependencies — explicit task ordering via the bitshift operator
    4. Connections & Variables — externalized configuration for
    portability

